In TypeScript, you can use the "as" operator to tell the type system that you know a value will only have a specific type, even though it may have multiple possible types in theory. Here's an example:

```typescript
let x: string | number;
x = "hello";
console.log(x.length); // This will work because x is a string
x = 42;
console.log(x.length); // This will throw an error because x is a number
```

In this example, the variable "x" is declared as having the type "string | number", which means it can hold either a string or a number. However, when we assign the value "hello" to "x", we use the "as" operator to tell the type system that we know "x" will only have the type "string" from that point forward. This allows us to call the "length" property on "x" without getting an error, because the type system knows that "x" is a string.

